        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: పార",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పార (pāra) — \"a spade\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పార (pāra) is a spade — a broad iron blade on a handle, for digging and for shifting బురద or ఇసుక."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the tool of the రైతు out of season: clearing a channel, banking up the edge of a పొలం, cutting a way for water to run."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word is inherited and it stays narrow. It does not stretch into anything figurative at all, which by itself says how firmly it belongs to the ground."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పార (pāra) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil pārai and Kannada pāre (DEDR 4093). DEDR lists Telugu pāra, \"spade\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three. The first one here you push rather than swing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
