          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "సుత్తి (sutti) — \"a hammer\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "సుత్తి (sutti) is a hammer: an iron head on a short handle, for driving things in and for beating things straight."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu turned it into a word about people. Somebody who makes the same point until you give in is doing సుత్తి (sutti), and telling them to stop the సుత్తి (sutti) is telling them to stop going on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In a city that second sense is now the commoner one, which is worth knowing before somebody uses it about you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "సుత్తి (sutti) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil cutti, Kannada suttige and Malayalam cutti (DEDR 2668). DEDR gives the Telugu as sutte."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four. The one whose second meaning you are likelier to hear."
            }
          ]
        },
