        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: కర్ర",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "కర్ర (karra) — \"a stick\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "కర్ర (karra) is a stick — a length of wood, cut or picked up, for everything the other four are too particular for."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is inherited and completely ordinary: a walking stick for a తాత, a herding stick behind a మేక, the handle still waiting for a గొడ్డలి (goḍḍali) head."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is also the right thing to ask for first. దయచేసి and a కర్ర (karra) costs nobody anything, and by the time you want the సుత్తి (sutti) you already know how to ask."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "కర్ర (karra) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil kaṟal, \"firewood\" (DEDR 1389), which gives the older Telugu spelling kaṟṟa, \"wood, stick\". DEDR compares Prakrit karālī, \"toothstick\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: గొడ్డలి (goḍḍali), రంపం (rampaṁ), పార (pāra), సుత్తి (sutti), కర్ర (karra). Enough to ask for anything in a shed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
